**A correlation id is a unique id assigned to a request at the edge and passed along to every service, queue message and log line involved in handling it, so you can pull up everything that happened for that one request. Today the W3C trace context `traceparent` header usually plays this role: its trace id is the correlation id.**

## How it flows
```text
client -> edge/gateway: generate id (or accept a trusted incoming one)
        -> service A: read header, store in request context, add to every log line
            -> HTTP call to B: forward header (traceparent / x-request-id)
            -> publish message: put id in message headers
                 -> worker: extract from headers, store in context, log with it
```

## Implementation in Node.js
```ts
import { AsyncLocalStorage } from "node:async_hooks";
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";

const ctx = new AsyncLocalStorage<{ requestId: string }>();

app.use((req, res, next) => {
  const requestId = req.get("x-request-id") ?? randomUUID();
  res.setHeader("x-request-id", requestId);          // return it so users can quote it to support
  ctx.run({ requestId }, next);
});

export const currentRequestId = () => ctx.getStore()?.requestId;
// the logger adds currentRequestId() to every line; the HTTP client adds it to outgoing headers
```

With OpenTelemetry you rarely write this yourself: auto-instrumentation propagates `traceparent` on HTTP and many messaging clients, and log instrumentation injects `trace_id`/`span_id` into each line.

## Correlation id vs trace id
- A **correlation id** is just a shared identifier; it groups logs.
- A **trace id** is part of a richer model: each hop also has a span id and parent, so you get a timed causal tree, not just a bag of related lines. Using the trace id as the correlation id gives you both.

## Gotchas
- **Do not trust arbitrary ids from the internet** blindly: validate format and length (it ends up in logs and headers), or generate at your own edge.
- **Async boundaries:** put the id in queue message headers, job payload metadata and scheduled job context. Retries should keep the original id (plus an attempt number).
- **Fan-out:** a batch job processing many requests has many ids; log the batch id and use span links.
- **Return it** in the response header so a user or support ticket can reference the exact request.

**Inject the trace context into the message headers when producing (a PRODUCER span), extract it when consuming and start a CONSUMER span whose parent, or link, is the producer's context. Use parent-child for one-message-one-job processing and span links for batch consumption. Many OpenTelemetry messaging instrumentations do this automatically, but you must verify it end to end, including retries and dead-letter queues.**

## Producer
```ts
import { context, propagation, trace, SpanKind } from "@opentelemetry/api";
const tracer = trace.getTracer("orders-svc");

await tracer.startActiveSpan("order.created publish", { kind: SpanKind.PRODUCER }, async (span) => {
  const headers: Record<string, string> = {};
  propagation.inject(context.active(), headers);        // writes traceparent (+ tracestate, baggage)
  await producer.send({ topic: "order.created", messages: [{ key: orderId, value, headers }] });
  span.end();
});
```

## Consumer
```ts
import { ROOT_CONTEXT } from "@opentelemetry/api";

await consumer.run({
  eachMessage: async ({ message }) => {
    const carrier = Object.fromEntries(
      Object.entries(message.headers ?? {}).map(([k, v]) => [k, v?.toString() ?? ""]),
    );
    const parentCtx = propagation.extract(ROOT_CONTEXT, carrier);
    await tracer.startActiveSpan("order.created process", { kind: SpanKind.CONSUMER }, parentCtx, async (span) => {
      try { await sendConfirmationEmail(message); } finally { span.end(); }
    });
  },
});
```

## Parent vs link
- **Parent-child:** one message, one unit of work. The trace reads naturally: HTTP request, publish, process, email API call.
- **Links:** a consumer that processes a batch of 100 messages in one span cannot have 100 parents, so it links to each producer context. Links also suit cases where the consumer's work is logically a new operation (e.g. a nightly job triggered by many events).

## Things to get right
- **Queue wait time:** record publish timestamp (from the message or broker) and compute lag at consume time as an attribute and a histogram metric; consumer group lag is the USE saturation signal.
- **Retries:** each delivery attempt is a new consumer span with an attempt count; keep the original headers on re-publish.
- **Dead-letter queues:** preserve headers so a replay joins the original trace.
- **Sampling:** the consumer follows the propagated sampled flag; tail sampling cannot wait minutes for a trace to finish, so async-heavy flows usually rely on head or parent-based decisions plus error capture.
- **Long gaps:** traces spanning minutes are fine for backends, but dashboards should show queue lag separately.
- **Logs** in the consumer pick up the trace id from the active span, linking worker logs to the original request.
